# Workforce identity and assessment readiness (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-08), verbatim:

> **Hard Rule: provide all the tools and systems on line for organizational
> assessment readiness. Auditor Binder provides current state of the programs we
> provide. Provide employee and contractor welcome letters explaining training
> and attestation, providing grading, required (if any) testing, reminders for
> uncompleted tasks, etc. Each employee and contractor has an identity in the
> system if they are covered by the regulatory requirements. Not every employee
> and contractor is required to be in the system as some are not performing
> regulated activities.**
>
> **Currently we are using only local accounts. We will also be using outside
> identity management systems already available but not used in the current
> enclaves.**

This is the workforce half of
[`complete-coverage-and-online-evidence.mdc`](complete-coverage-and-online-evidence.mdc).
That rule says every obligation needs a resource, a workflow, and a record. This
one says who the record is about, how that person is identified, and what the
program owes them before it starts asking them to sign things.

## The rule

> **Assessment-readiness tooling is online in the product, not a document the
> customer maintains beside it. Every person covered by a regulatory requirement
> has a durable identity row in the system, carrying a recorded scope basis that
> says which regulated activity puts them in scope, who decided, and when.
> People who perform no regulated activity are deliberately out of scope, and
> that exclusion is itself a recorded decision. The roster row — not a login —
> is the identity that evidence binds to, so moving from local accounts to an
> external identity provider never rewrites attestation history. Programs open
> with a welcome letter that explains what is being asked, and where a program
> declares grading or testing, the score and the pass decision are part of the
> evidence record.**

## 1. Online, not alongside

Everything an organization needs to walk into an assessment is reachable in the
product: the roster and its scope basis, the program schedule, the documents,
the completion state, the attestation records, the reminder history, and the
binder that assembles them. If readiness requires a spreadsheet, a shared drive,
or an email thread, the product has not delivered readiness — see §2 of the
coverage rule for the evidence properties every record must carry.

## 2. The Auditor Binder shows live program state

> **Binder sections about programs this product runs are derived from live state
> at render time, never from an operator-typed snapshot.**

A completion percentage an operator typed into a text box is a claim. A
completion percentage computed from the assignment ledger at render time, with
the denominator explained, is evidence. Concretely, a binder section covering a
program we run must derive:

- the program, its cycle, and its due-date policy,
- the in-scope population and how the denominator was determined,
- completion, outstanding, and overdue counts as of the render timestamp,
- the attestation records backing those counts, addressable by person and
 document version,
- the reminder history for anyone still outstanding.

Honesty carries over unchanged: live numbers describe **delivery**, never
control satisfaction. A binder section may state that a program ran and that
records exist. It may not conclude that a control is Met — see
[`assessment-binder-section-content.mdc`](assessment-binder-section-content.mdc)
and [`compliance-claims-honesty.mdc`](compliance-claims-honesty.mdc).

## 3. Scope is a recorded determination, not an implied one

The directive is explicit that the roster is **not** the whole company. Only
people performing regulated activities belong in it. That makes roster
membership a scoping decision, and the first question any assessor asks about a
roster is *how do you know this list is complete?*

Every in-scope person carries:

| Field | Why |
| --- | --- |
| **Scope basis** | Which regulated activity puts them in scope — handles CUI, handles FCI, administers an in-scope system, has physical access to an in-scope area, approves access, and so on |
| **Determined by** | The identity of the person who made the call, resolved from a real principal |
| **Determined at** | UTC server timestamp |
| **Review cycle** | When the determination is next re-examined, because job duties change |

Exclusions are decisions too. When an organization states that a population
performs no regulated activity, that statement is recorded with its basis and
its author. An absent row must be distinguishable from a considered exclusion,
because "we never thought about them" and "we determined they never touch it"
are very different answers in an interview.

Scope basis is a **defensible boundary, not a loophole**. Narrowing scope to
shrink a completion denominator is exactly the misuse this field must make
visible, which is why the decision is attributed and timestamped.

## 4. Identity is IdP-agnostic by construction

The operator has stated the trajectory plainly: local accounts today, external
identity management later. That makes one design decision load-bearing.

> **The durable person record is the roster row. Authentication is a separate,
> replaceable binding. Evidence binds to the person record and denormalizes the
> signer's identity at signing time.**

Consequences that are not negotiable:

1. **Attestations reference the person record**, and additionally capture the
 signer's name, address, kind, and organization **as they were at signing**.
 A later email change, a rename, or a move to a federated subject cannot
 alter what an existing record says.
2. **A roster row is not an operator account.** Someone who only reads a
 document and attests never needs a console seat, a password, or a role. The
 low-friction path stays low-friction.
3. **Authentication bindings are additive and plural.** A person may be
 reachable by a single-use emailed link today, by a local account, and by an
 external provider subject later — including all three at once during a
 migration. The identity source is recorded per binding.
4. **External provider bindings are identified by issuer and subject**, not by
 email. Email is a delivery address and it changes; the issuer/subject pair
 is the durable claim.
5. **Federating an existing workforce is a linking operation**, never a
 re-import that creates new person rows. A migration that orphans prior
 attestations has destroyed the audit trail the program exists to produce.
6. **Federation never lowers the security floor** —
 [`baseline-security-perfect-always.mdc`](baseline-security-perfect-always.mdc)
 still binds, and an identity provider that does not return the claims we
 rely on is reported as a gap rather than papered over.

## 5. A program opens with a welcome letter

Before a program asks a person to sign anything, it tells them what is
happening. Employee and contractor variants both exist, because a contractor
needs to know whose rules apply and who to contact at their own employer.

A welcome letter states, in plain words:

- what this is and why they received it,
- what they are being asked to read and attest to,
- what attesting means — a legal statement about their own conduct,
- whether there is a knowledge check, how it is scored, and what passing is,
- how many attempts they get, and what happens if they do not pass,
- when it is due and what happens if it is late,
- who to ask for help, by name and contact,
- that their completion is recorded and retained.

Welcome letters are training materials, so they inherit the plain-language and
image rules in
[`training-materials-plain-language-and-images.mdc`](training-materials-plain-language-and-images.mdc):
sixth-grade reading level, no unexplained jargon, image rich, original or
open-source content only. A welcome letter written in compliance vocabulary
defeats its own purpose.

## 6. Grading and testing produce evidence, not vibes

Testing is optional per program. Where a program declares one, the result is
part of the record:

| Requirement | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| **Recorded score** | The numeric result, stored — not just a pass flag |
| **Declared threshold** | The passing score, captured as of the attempt, so later policy changes do not retroactively pass or fail anyone |
| **Attempt history** | Every attempt, with its own timestamp and score, append-only |
| **Attempt policy** | Maximum attempts and what happens on exhaustion, decided by the program and recorded on the assignment |
| **Question versioning** | The question set version the person actually saw, bound like a document hash |
| **Pass decision** | Derived from score and threshold at attempt time, stored, and never mutated |

"I have read and understood" with no check is an attestation, and it is
legitimate evidence of attestation. It is **not** a grade, and a completion
report must not present it as one.

## 7. Reminders are a logged obligation

Uncompleted tasks generate reminders on the program's ladder — welcome, then
reminders before due, then due, then overdue. Every send attempt writes an
append-only log row with recipient, kind, outcome, and failure reason.

**A reminder that is not logged did not happen.** When an assessor asks why
someone was six weeks late, "the system reminded them four times, here are the
timestamps" is the answer that holds up. A silent send failure that leaves no
row is a
[`silent-failure-is-a-bug.mdc`](silent-failure-is-a-bug.mdc) violation, and
claiming reminder coverage without observing a real send violates
[`mailpit-messaging-comprehensive-test.mdc`](mailpit-messaging-comprehensive-test.mdc).

## 8. Forbidden constructs

- Readiness that depends on a customer-maintained spreadsheet, shared drive, or
 email thread.
- A binder section about a program we run whose numbers were typed by an
 operator rather than derived at render time.
- A live completion percentage presented as, or adjacent to, a claim that a
 control is Met.
- A roster row with no recorded scope basis, or an exclusion with no recorded
 reason — including bulk imports that skip the determination.
- Narrowing scope to improve a completion statistic.
- Binding an attestation to a login, an email address, or an external provider
 subject instead of to the durable person record.
- Requiring an operator account, role, or console seat for a person whose only
 task is to read and attest.
- Identifying a federated person by email rather than by issuer and subject.
- A federation migration that re-imports the workforce and creates new person
 rows, orphaning prior attestations.
- Presenting an unscored acknowledgement as a grade or a test result.
- A pass decision recomputed from current policy rather than read from the
 stored attempt.
- Mutating a score, an attempt, an attestation, or a notification log row in
 place.
- Reminder sends that leave no log row, or a claim that reminders work without
 an observed message in a real sink.
- Describing any of the above as complete because the schema exists. Schema is
 not delivery — see §9.

